public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        Set<Id> ids = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id contactId : contactIds) {
            if (contactId != null) {
                ids.add(contactId);
            }
        }

        if (ids.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :ids
            WITH USER_MODE
        ]) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)) {
                c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
                contactsToUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.update(contactsToUpdate, false, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);

        for (Database.SaveResult result : results) {
            if (!result.isSuccess()) {
                List<Database.Error> errors = result.getErrors();
                if (errors != null && !errors.isEmpty()) {
                    failures.put(result.getId(), errors[0].getMessage());
                } else {
                    failures.put(result.getId(), 'Unknown error');
                }
            }
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

Notes on changes from the stated approach:
- I used `WITH USER_MODE` and `AccessLevel.USER_MODE` to enforce CRUD/FLS, which is current Salesforce security best practice for Apex. If this utility must run in system mode, remove `WITH USER_MODE` and use `Database.update(contactsToUpdate, false)` instead.
- I deduplicated input Ids and filtered nulls to avoid redundant query rows and unexpected behavior.
- I treated blank/whitespace `Phone` as “no phone” via `String.isNotBlank`, so those contacts are left out.
- I skipped the DML call entirely when there is nothing to update, avoiding an unnecessary `Database.update` call.
- The regex `[^0-9+]` removes every character except ASCII digits and `+`, exactly as requested.

