```apex
/**
 * CasePriorityHandler
 *
 * Business rule:
 *   - A new Case whose Account has Rating = 'Hot' is created with Priority = 'High'.
 *   - Every other Case (non-Hot Account, or no Account at all) keeps the Priority
 *     it was created with.
 *
 * Called from CaseTrigger (before insert).
 * Bulk-safe: exactly one SOQL query per trigger execution, regardless of batch size.
 */
public without sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    /** Account rating that makes a new Case jump the queue. */
    private static final String HOT_RATING = 'Hot';

    /** Priority assigned to Cases created on Hot Accounts. */
    private static final String HOT_PRIORITY = 'High';

    /**
     * Sets Priority = 'High' on any Case in the batch whose Account is rated 'Hot'.
     *
     * @param newCases Trigger.new from CaseTrigger (before insert). Modified in place.
     */
    public static void setPriorityOnInsert(List<Case> newCases) {
        if (newCases == null || newCases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 1. Collect the Accounts referenced by this batch (skip Cases with no Account).
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }
        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. One query for the whole batch: which of those Accounts are Hot?
        Set<Id> hotAccountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Account a : [
            SELECT Id
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
              AND Rating = :HOT_RATING
        ]) {
            hotAccountIds.add(a.Id);
        }
        if (hotAccountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 3. Apply the rule. Cases that aren't matched keep whatever Priority they came in with.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null && hotAccountIds.contains(c.AccountId)) {
                c.Priority = HOT_PRIORITY;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## What I did differently from the request, and why

**1. I did not put `[SELECT Rating FROM Account WHERE Id = :c.AccountId]` inside the `for` loop.**

This is the one deviation that matters. Your ERP integration inserts up to 200 Cases in a single transaction, and a trigger executes once for the whole batch. A query inside the loop would therefore run up to 200 times in one execution:

- Salesforce's synchronous SOQL limit is **100 queries per transaction**. The batch would start failing somewhere around Case #101 with `System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries: 101`, and because trigger exceptions are unhandled here, **the entire 200-Case batch would fail and roll back** — including the cases that were fine. Email-to-Case would be unaffected (one Case per execution), so this would look like an intermittent "ERP is broken" bug rather than a code bug.
- Even below the limit, it's 200 round trips where 1 would do.

The bulkified version produces **identical results** to what you asked for — Hot Account → `High`, everything else untouched — with one query per batch. The `Set<Id>` lookup is a well-known pattern, so it's still easy for an admin to read: gather Account Ids, ask which are Hot, apply.

I also select only `Id` instead of `Rating`, since the `WHERE` clause already filters to Hot Accounts and we only need to know *which* Accounts matched.

**2. `public without sharing` on the handler.**

`CaseTrigger` can be fired by Email-to-Case, by the ERP integration user, or by a person in the UI. I don't want "did this Case get triaged?" to depend on whether the running user happens to have read access to the Account. Declaring `without sharing` makes the behaviour deterministic. Note that Apex triggers themselves run in system mode, so this is mainly about removing ambiguity for whoever reads the class next. If your security model requires the running user's Account sharing to be respected, change the keyword to `with sharing` — the logic is unchanged, but a user who can't see the Account will get a Case left at its default Priority.

**3. No other behaviour changed.** Cases with no `AccountId`, and Cases whose Account is Warm/Cold/blank, are left exactly as created. I deliberately don't write `c.Priority` for them, so an integration that sends `Priority = 'Medium'` or `'Low'` keeps it.

**Two things to be aware of (not code changes, just flags):**

- This is a **before insert** rule, so it only sees an `AccountId` that is already populated when the Case is inserted. If any of your channels set the Account *after* insert (some Email-to-Case matching setups, or a record-triggered flow), those Cases will not be promoted and you'd need to add an `after insert`/`after update` path on `Case`.
- Deploying this needs a test class (75% coverage on `Case`). It should cover: Hot Account → `High`; non-Hot Account → unchanged; no Account → unchanged; and a 200-record bulk insert asserting the SOQL limit isn't hit. Tell me if you want me to write it.

